      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=-2x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={5}^{u}\\ln(5),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={5}^{u}\\ln(5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ & =(-2)\\cdot {5}^{u}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =-2\\cdot {5}^{-2x-2}\\ln(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(-2)\\cdot {5}^{-2(0)-2}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =-2\\cdot {5}^{-2}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{25}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={5}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{25})&=(- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{25})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{2 x \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{25} + \\frac{1}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
